WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.465 --> 00:00:01.781
- If you're going to work tomorrow

00:00:01.781 --> 00:00:03.982
because that's what you did yesterday

00:00:03.982 --> 00:00:06.687
you're not going to be as good
tomorrow as you were yesterday.

00:00:06.687 --> 00:00:08.935
I never had anybody
say something like that

00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:10.530
to me, "You could be a great one.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.260
"You could be the national champion."

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.765
Some people are about as committed as

00:00:13.765 --> 00:00:16.279
a kamikaze pilot on his 39th mission.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:18.949
When I was boy, during the Depression,

00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:21.053
we lived next door to some rich folks.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.705
I knew they were rich for two reasons.

00:00:22.705 --> 00:00:23.674
Number one they had a cook.

00:00:23.674 --> 00:00:25.842
Number two, the cook
had something to cook.

00:00:25.842 --> 00:00:28.067
Where would my integrity
be if I came to you

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:30.705
unprepared to make
something, a presentation

00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:33.487
that could make a difference in your life?

00:00:33.487 --> 00:00:36.661
And, when I choose to eat too much today

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:40.078
I have chosen to weigh too much tomorrow.

00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:44.371
You see, most people
never dare to evaluate

00:00:44.371 --> 00:00:46.802
really where they are.

00:00:46.802 --> 00:00:50.420
"Zig," they tell me, "you could
sell anything to anybody."

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.068
And I said, "Yeah, they lied to ya."

00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:54.949
So many times the employee
goes to the employer

00:00:54.949 --> 00:00:56.272
and say, "Give me a raise then I'll start

00:00:56.272 --> 00:00:57.805
"coming to work on time."

00:00:57.805 --> 00:01:00.020
- He was an American author, salesman,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.486
and motivational speaker.

00:01:01.486 --> 00:01:04.433
He served in United States
Navy during World War II

00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:05.933
from 1943 to 1945.

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:10.903
In 1968 he became Vice
President and Training Director

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:13.388
of the Automotive Performance Company.

00:01:13.388 --> 00:01:15.978
He's Zig Zigler and here's my take on his

00:01:15.978 --> 00:01:17.569
Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:01:17.569 --> 00:01:20.815
(air rushes)
(thuds)

00:01:20.815 --> 00:01:22.843
- When you're asleep,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:01:22.843 --> 00:01:24.593
you need your dreams.

00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:28.274
I'm here to tell you that
when you're wide awake

00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:30.441
you also need your dreams.

00:01:32.089 --> 00:01:33.892
You must have your goals.

00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:35.767
You'll never make it is
a wandering generality.

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:38.864
You must become a meaningful specific.

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:40.216
If you're going to work tomorrow

00:01:40.216 --> 00:01:42.450
because that's what you did yesterday

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.252
you're not going to be
as good tomorrow as you

00:01:44.252 --> 00:01:47.027
were yesterday because
now you're two days older

00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:51.494
and no closer to the goal
which you do not have.

00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:54.861
You can't make it as
a wandering generality

00:01:54.861 --> 00:01:57.494
- Tell us about some of
the jobs that you've held

00:01:57.494 --> 00:01:59.621
over the years selling
and what you've sold

00:01:59.621 --> 00:02:01.507
and some of the lessons
that you've learned

00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:03.122
in selling over the years.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.494
- Well, I sold heavy
duty waterless cookware

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:08.550
on the door-to-door demonstration plan.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.322
And, that was a marvelous experience.

00:02:10.322 --> 00:02:13.252
It took me the first two and a half years

00:02:13.252 --> 00:02:15.468
I struggled financially.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.277
Now, that doesn't mean I didn't sell a lot

00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:18.268
because I did.

00:02:18.268 --> 00:02:19.760
I sold my furniture, sold my car.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.499
(laughs)

00:02:21.499 --> 00:02:24.200
And, that's fairly close to
the truth, it really was.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.503
Then I went to a meeting
and a man named P.C. Merrill

00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:31.312
who's an American Indian persuaded me that

00:02:31.312 --> 00:02:33.504
I could be the number
one salesman in America

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:36.483
out of the 7,000 member sales force.

00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:38.692
He said, "You've got
everything that it takes

00:02:38.692 --> 00:02:41.515
"to be a great salesman
if you just believed in

00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:44.551
"yourself and went to work
on a regular schedule."

00:02:44.551 --> 00:02:45.737
- And, this is in a cookware business?

00:02:45.737 --> 00:02:47.773
- That's right, and I
put on the demonstration

00:02:47.773 --> 00:02:49.799
on door to door sales and so forth.

00:02:49.799 --> 00:02:52.764
I'd never had anybody say
something like that to me.

00:02:52.764 --> 00:02:53.964
"You could be a great one, you could be

00:02:53.964 --> 00:02:55.043
"the national champion."

00:02:55.043 --> 00:02:57.569
Now, I've always been loved, but nobody

00:02:57.569 --> 00:02:59.961
ever thought of me as a champion.

00:02:59.961 --> 00:03:02.919
Because of Mister Merrill's integrity

00:03:02.919 --> 00:03:04.853
and because he had written
the training program

00:03:04.853 --> 00:03:07.170
and because he had set all of the records

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.131
I took him absolutely at his word.

00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:14.109
The previous year I had
not been in the top 5,000

00:03:14.109 --> 00:03:17.174
out that 7,000 member sales force.

00:03:17.174 --> 00:03:19.460
But, the year after
Mister Merrill got through

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.278
with me I was number two out of the 7,000,

00:03:23.278 --> 00:03:26.959
had received the best promotion that

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:30.054
was available in the company at that time.

00:03:30.054 --> 00:03:32.322
And, the interesting
thing about the success

00:03:32.322 --> 00:03:35.969
that year he did not really
teach me that much about sales.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:38.645
I'd been in it two and
half years at that point,

00:03:38.645 --> 00:03:41.907
but what he did teach
me was about who I was.

00:03:41.907 --> 00:03:44.539
The picture I had of myself
changed dramatically.

00:03:44.539 --> 00:03:47.116
I started thinking as a
champion and performing

00:03:47.116 --> 00:03:48.285
as a champion.

00:03:48.285 --> 00:03:49.866
And, I did finish number two and had

00:03:49.866 --> 00:03:51.615
that wonderful promotion.

00:03:51.615 --> 00:03:54.865
Commitment plays heavily in everything.

00:03:55.859 --> 00:03:58.970
If you've made a commitment when you hit

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.142
the rough spots, not if,
when you hit the rough spots

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:06.345
your immediate thought is,
"How can I solve the problem?"

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:08.636
If you have not made the
commitment when you hit

00:04:08.636 --> 00:04:11.217
the rough spot whether it's
in marriage or education

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.450
or career or whatever
your first thought is,

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.088
"Man, how can I get out of this deal?"

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:19.488
And so, that commitment plays a huge role.

00:04:19.488 --> 00:04:21.936
And, some people are about as committed

00:04:21.936 --> 00:04:24.769
as a kamikaze pilot on it's 39th mission.

00:04:24.769 --> 00:04:28.634
I mean, they don't really
take it that seriously.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.662
I was raised in the little town
of Yazoo City, Mississippi.

00:04:31.662 --> 00:04:33.394
Now, I know a lot of folks
go 'round the country

00:04:33.394 --> 00:04:36.094
trying to impress people by
claiming to be from Yazoo City.

00:04:36.094 --> 00:04:37.257
(laughs)

00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:40.841
But, I really am and
when I was a boy during

00:04:40.841 --> 00:04:43.926
the Depression we lived next
door to some rich folks.

00:04:43.926 --> 00:04:45.434
I knew they were rich for two reasons.

00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:46.380
Number on they had a cook.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.754
Number two the cook had something to cook.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:50.854
(laughs)

00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:53.435
During the Depression that
was a sure sign of wealth.

00:04:53.435 --> 00:04:55.577
I was over there for lunch
one day, as I tried to be

00:04:55.577 --> 00:04:57.161
everyday,
(laughs)

00:04:57.161 --> 00:04:59.059
and don't misunderstand that.

00:04:59.059 --> 00:05:01.119
Even though there was a
Depression on we certainly

00:05:01.119 --> 00:05:03.188
had plenty to eat at my house.

00:05:03.188 --> 00:05:05.274
I know we had plenty
because if I ever passed

00:05:05.274 --> 00:05:07.811
my plate for seconds
they'd always tell me,

00:05:07.811 --> 00:05:09.360
"No, you've had plenty."

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.618
So, I know we had plenty.

00:05:11.618 --> 00:05:13.246
Now, the cook brought the biscuits out,

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:15.398
and this is not an exaggeration,

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:19.517
those biscuits were not
as thick as my wrist was.

00:05:19.517 --> 00:05:21.234
And, I looked at 'em for
a moment, I said Maude,

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:23.231
what in the world
happened to your biscuits?

00:05:23.231 --> 00:05:25.197
She reared back gave a big old tummy laugh

00:05:25.197 --> 00:05:27.161
and said, "Well, I'll tell
you about those biscuits."

00:05:27.161 --> 00:05:30.888
She said, "They squatted
to rise," but she said

00:05:30.888 --> 00:05:33.611
they just got cooked in the squat.

00:05:33.611 --> 00:05:35.364
(laughs)

00:05:35.364 --> 00:05:37.825
You anybody that's getting
cooked in the squat?

00:05:37.825 --> 00:05:39.292
You know anybody who's
going to do something

00:05:39.292 --> 00:05:42.087
just as soon as or they're
half a mind to do such

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:42.920
and such a thing?

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:44.680
You ever have anybody say,
"Well, wait till the kids

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.368
"get out of school and then
I'll really get involved

00:05:46.368 --> 00:05:47.253
"in this project.

00:05:47.253 --> 00:05:49.081
"We got so many things going on right now,

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:50.155
"wait till they're out of school.

00:05:50.155 --> 00:05:51.420
"Wait till the summer time comes then I'll

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.656
"really get busy."

00:05:53.656 --> 00:05:55.682
The kids get out of school
you know what they say then?

00:05:55.682 --> 00:05:57.254
"Well, I didn't realize
it but I got to take

00:05:57.254 --> 00:05:59.495
"the kids somewhere every day.

00:05:59.495 --> 00:06:00.933
"I had more time when they were in school.

00:06:00.933 --> 00:06:02.048
"Wait till they get
back in school and then

00:06:02.048 --> 00:06:03.016
"I'll really get busy."

00:06:03.016 --> 00:06:05.100
Kids get back in school,
you know what they say then?

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.192
"Well, for the first time in
19 years Dearhold Centralized

00:06:08.192 --> 00:06:10.105
"finally got a winning
football team and you

00:06:10.105 --> 00:06:11.251
"got to support the kids there.

00:06:11.251 --> 00:06:12.434
"Wait till after the football season's

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:14.149
"then I'll really get busy."

00:06:14.149 --> 00:06:16.188
Football season ends, you
know what they say then?

00:06:16.188 --> 00:06:17.763
"Well, here it is
Thanksgiving and Christmas

00:06:17.763 --> 00:06:19.574
"and New Years and people
don't want to be bothered

00:06:19.574 --> 00:06:21.042
"with this sort of thing
this time of the year.

00:06:21.042 --> 00:06:22.447
"Wait until the after the
first of the year, man,

00:06:22.447 --> 00:06:24.497
"then I'll really get busy."

00:06:24.497 --> 00:06:26.616
After the first of the year
you know what they say?

00:06:26.616 --> 00:06:28.432
"Well, it's the weather.

00:06:28.432 --> 00:06:31.425
"Did you ever see weather
like this in Chicago,

00:06:31.425 --> 00:06:33.693
"or San Francisco or Dallas or wherever.

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:35.543
"Wait till the weather
settles down and then

00:06:35.543 --> 00:06:37.011
"I'll really get busy.

00:06:37.011 --> 00:06:38.383
"Oh, I know what you're thinking.

00:06:38.383 --> 00:06:40.141
"You're thinking I've lost interest in it,

00:06:40.141 --> 00:06:41.338
"but that's not the way I do things.

00:06:41.338 --> 00:06:44.050
"Just let me tell you
the way I do things now.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.086
"Here's the way I am, see,
this is just the way I do it.

00:06:47.086 --> 00:06:49.836
(crowd applauds)

00:06:56.487 --> 00:06:59.355
"See, the way I do things is
I get everything organized.

00:06:59.355 --> 00:07:01.563
"I get it lined up, I
have everything in just

00:07:01.563 --> 00:07:04.194
"such and such order and
then man, once I get it

00:07:04.194 --> 00:07:06.614
"all put together that's
when I really get after it.

00:07:06.614 --> 00:07:08.784
"I mean, that's just,
I've always been that way,

00:07:08.784 --> 00:07:09.971
"do things that way.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:11.345
"I know some of these
other folks are ahead

00:07:11.345 --> 00:07:13.578
"of me right now but you
just wait, I'll catch 'em.

00:07:13.578 --> 00:07:16.323
"But this weather it is just so horrible.

00:07:16.323 --> 00:07:17.305
"Wait till it clears up."

00:07:17.305 --> 00:07:19.112
And, the weather clears up and
you know what they say then?

00:07:19.112 --> 00:07:22.154
"Well, here it is it's
Easter time and Easter time

00:07:22.154 --> 00:07:23.254
"you know that's a family time

00:07:23.254 --> 00:07:24.976
"and we always spend a
lot of time together.

00:07:24.976 --> 00:07:27.375
"And, you said so yourself,
the family's extremely

00:07:27.375 --> 00:07:29.555
"important to you, you do
the work for the family

00:07:29.555 --> 00:07:31.372
"and if you can't spend
some time with them

00:07:31.372 --> 00:07:32.889
"you might as well not do all the work.

00:07:32.889 --> 00:07:34.368
"I mean, wait till
after Easter's over then

00:07:34.368 --> 00:07:35.611
"I'll really get busy."

00:07:35.611 --> 00:07:37.452
And then, after Easter
you know what they say?

00:07:37.452 --> 00:07:39.973
"At long last, we've got
some beautiful weather

00:07:39.973 --> 00:07:41.786
"and I haven't hit a
golf ball or wet a hook

00:07:41.786 --> 00:07:43.487
"in I don't know when.

00:07:43.487 --> 00:07:46.131
"Man, I got to have some
r and r, I've got to have

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:47.574
"some rest and relaxation.

00:07:47.574 --> 00:07:49.638
"And, you said you
can't work all the time.

00:07:49.638 --> 00:07:51.686
"I mean, an individual needs
some time with themself.

00:07:51.686 --> 00:07:53.507
"Wait till I relax a little bit and man,

00:07:53.507 --> 00:07:55.666
"then I'll really get after it."

00:07:55.666 --> 00:07:57.451
And finally, they get
after it a little bit

00:07:57.451 --> 00:07:59.422
or when they have their fun and relaxation

00:07:59.422 --> 00:08:03.589
then they say, "Well, it's
almost time for the kids

00:08:05.263 --> 00:08:07.471
"to get out of school."

00:08:07.471 --> 00:08:09.280
And, that's where we came in.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.600
As we say down home
folks, you can put this

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.258
in your pipe and smoke
it 'cause it's the truth.

00:08:14.258 --> 00:08:17.187
The people who wait for
Aunt Matilda to move out

00:08:17.187 --> 00:08:19.823
or John to get onto the
day shift or the new models

00:08:19.823 --> 00:08:23.186
to come out or the new
mayor to come in or for

00:08:23.186 --> 00:08:25.763
the new advertising
campaign to get started

00:08:25.763 --> 00:08:28.424
the people who wait on the
new senator in Washington

00:08:28.424 --> 00:08:31.467
or until inflation slows or
the rate of interest come down,

00:08:31.467 --> 00:08:35.496
the people who wait for
changes to take place out there

00:08:35.496 --> 00:08:38.396
before they do the changing in here

00:08:38.396 --> 00:08:43.210
are flat going to end up
getting cooked in the squat.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:47.293
The rule is simple, you
do it now, you do it now.

00:08:49.443 --> 00:08:52.550
How many of you feel like
I've made this talk before?

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.765
Can I see your hand please?

00:08:54.765 --> 00:08:57.818
Several hundred times,
I made it yesterday.

00:08:57.818 --> 00:09:00.314
You know what I did between
yesterday and today?

00:09:00.314 --> 00:09:03.912
I spent over six hours
getting ready for today.

00:09:03.912 --> 00:09:06.800
You see, I think it would
be arrogant if I thought

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.002
I could stand up and
spit it out just because

00:09:09.002 --> 00:09:12.287
I did it yesterday or hundreds of times.

00:09:12.287 --> 00:09:15.251
That's arrogance, that's
when Buster Douglas

00:09:15.251 --> 00:09:17.303
knocks out Mike Tyson.

00:09:17.303 --> 00:09:19.516
That's when an expansion team in Houston

00:09:19.516 --> 00:09:22.931
beats an established NFL team in Dallas.

00:09:22.931 --> 00:09:26.316
I dare not, look at the
people here, several thousand.

00:09:26.316 --> 00:09:27.604
I'm taking over an hour.

00:09:27.604 --> 00:09:30.395
That's several thousands hours of time.

00:09:30.395 --> 00:09:32.961
Where would my integrity be if I came here

00:09:32.961 --> 00:09:35.512
unprepared to make
something, a presentation

00:09:35.512 --> 00:09:38.404
that could make a difference in your life.

00:09:38.404 --> 00:09:41.341
There is no way, you got to prepare for it

00:09:41.341 --> 00:09:42.542
ladies and gentlemen.

00:09:42.542 --> 00:09:45.190
Almost did not go to the
most important sales meeting

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.123
I would ever have missed.

00:09:47.123 --> 00:09:50.272
That morning I awakened, I'd been up there

00:09:50.272 --> 00:09:51.738
in Charlotte all day long in a meeting

00:09:51.738 --> 00:09:53.464
and didn't learn anything.

00:09:53.464 --> 00:09:55.490
Five o'clock the alarm
clock goes off and it was

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.551
an alarm clock in those days,
now it's an opportunity clock.

00:09:58.551 --> 00:10:00.834
If I can hear that it means
I've got an opportunity

00:10:00.834 --> 00:10:01.667
to get up and go.

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:02.763
If I can't hear it that might mean I

00:10:02.763 --> 00:10:03.846
done got up and gone.

00:10:03.846 --> 00:10:06.188
Well, anyhow,
(laughs)

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:07.754
went off, we were living
in little three room

00:10:07.754 --> 00:10:10.442
apartment above a grocery
store, five o'clock

00:10:10.442 --> 00:10:12.845
the weather was horrible.

00:10:12.845 --> 00:10:15.359
When the clock went off
I looked out and I said

00:10:15.359 --> 00:10:17.666
no sane person would ever get in a little

00:10:17.666 --> 00:10:20.034
Crosley automobile without
a heater in weather

00:10:20.034 --> 00:10:22.274
like this and drive all
the way to Charlotte

00:10:22.274 --> 00:10:23.600
to go to another meeting.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.381
So, I lay back down.

00:10:25.381 --> 00:10:27.521
As I lay down the words
of my mother came back

00:10:27.521 --> 00:10:31.226
to me, "Son, if you work
for a man you work for him

00:10:31.226 --> 00:10:35.072
"all the way, you're
loyal to him in every way.

00:10:35.072 --> 00:10:38.253
"And, if your word is no
good, you're no good."

00:10:38.253 --> 00:10:40.083
I had promised this
company, which had taken me

00:10:40.083 --> 00:10:42.626
two months to get the job
that if they would give me

00:10:42.626 --> 00:10:45.433
the opportunity I'd be
at every sales meeting,

00:10:45.433 --> 00:10:47.169
I'd be at every training session.

00:10:47.169 --> 00:10:49.724
And, in two and a half
years, despite my lack

00:10:49.724 --> 00:10:52.428
of success, I had been
at every sales meeting,

00:10:52.428 --> 00:10:55.717
at every training session
and I'd never even been late.

00:10:55.717 --> 00:10:57.974
The words of my mother came back.

00:10:57.974 --> 00:10:59.741
I rolled out of bed, went to the meeting,

00:10:59.741 --> 00:11:02.741
that's the one that changed my life.

00:11:03.885 --> 00:11:04.989
- How did it change your life?

00:11:04.989 --> 00:11:06.531
- It changed my life
because that's the day

00:11:06.531 --> 00:11:08.520
that Mister Merrill persuaded me that

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.022
I could be the champion.

00:11:11.022 --> 00:11:11.855
- Comes full circle now.

00:11:11.855 --> 00:11:13.863
- Ah man, it sure did

00:11:13.863 --> 00:11:16.967
and so it made all the
difference in the world.

00:11:16.967 --> 00:11:19.334
I want to talk a little bit
about goals on the light of

00:11:19.334 --> 00:11:23.378
how do you lose 37
pounds and write a book.

00:11:23.378 --> 00:11:25.837
I'm just going to kind of to
give you that as an example.

00:11:25.837 --> 00:11:29.869
For 24 years of my adult life, by choice,

00:11:29.869 --> 00:11:32.586
I weighed well over 200 pounds.

00:11:32.586 --> 00:11:35.497
I say by choice because,
you see, I have never

00:11:35.497 --> 00:11:37.621
accidentally eaten anything.

00:11:37.621 --> 00:11:39.704
(laughs)

00:11:42.442 --> 00:11:45.050
I mean, it's always been deliberate.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:48.212
And, when I choose to eat too much today

00:11:48.212 --> 00:11:51.629
I have chosen to weigh too much tomorrow.

00:11:53.393 --> 00:11:57.560
You can choose to set goals
and realize your potential

00:11:58.887 --> 00:12:00.953
or you can choose not set them.

00:12:00.953 --> 00:12:03.473
Now, if you choose not to set
them you got to understand

00:12:03.473 --> 00:12:06.446
that the consequences
are not going to be good

00:12:06.446 --> 00:12:08.414
down the road.

00:12:08.414 --> 00:12:11.891
For 24 years I was going
to lose that weight.

00:12:11.891 --> 00:12:14.584
As a matter of fact in
24 years I lost several

00:12:14.584 --> 00:12:16.681
thousand pounds of weight.

00:12:16.681 --> 00:12:19.630
How many of you already
know what I'm talking about.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:23.612
But, it wasn't until I
wrote it down, put a date

00:12:23.612 --> 00:12:27.872
on it, listed the obstacles
I had to overcome,

00:12:27.872 --> 00:12:30.184
identified the people, the
groups, the organizations

00:12:30.184 --> 00:12:35.099
I needed to work with,
spelled out a plan of action,

00:12:35.099 --> 00:12:38.757
set that time limit in
there, and identified

00:12:38.757 --> 00:12:42.951
all of the benefits to me,
it as only when I did that

00:12:42.951 --> 00:12:47.118
that the goal became a
reality and I lost the weight.

00:12:48.139 --> 00:12:50.736
For 10 or 15 years I was
going to write a book.

00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:52.741
You know anybody who's a
going to do just a whole

00:12:52.741 --> 00:12:54.029
lot of things, folks?

00:12:54.029 --> 00:12:55.144
I was going to write a book.

00:12:55.144 --> 00:12:59.371
But, it wasn't until I got busy
writing the book and writing

00:12:59.371 --> 00:13:03.549
the plans first before the
book ever materialized.

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:04.932
Now, if I sound like
I'm trying to sell you

00:13:04.932 --> 00:13:08.242
on having goals, how many of
you are getting close already?

00:13:08.242 --> 00:13:09.911
How many of you are becoming convinced

00:13:09.911 --> 00:13:12.820
right quick like that you
need to have those goals?

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:14.311
There's no question about it.

00:13:14.311 --> 00:13:16.727
The immortal J.C. Penny,
many, many years ago

00:13:16.727 --> 00:13:20.060
said, "Give me a stock clerk with a goal

00:13:21.111 --> 00:13:24.163
"and I'll give you a
man who'll make history.

00:13:24.163 --> 00:13:28.008
"But give me a man without a goal and I'll

00:13:28.008 --> 00:13:30.091
"give you a stock clerk."

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.777
Now, the interesting thing is goals

00:13:32.777 --> 00:13:34.646
do not care who has them.

00:13:34.646 --> 00:13:37.479
Let me give you a classic
example of the way they work.

00:13:37.479 --> 00:13:40.562
In 1950, a war torn devastated Japan,

00:13:42.589 --> 00:13:45.676
a nation who had lost an
incredibly high percentage of

00:13:45.676 --> 00:13:48.700
it's young man, their
cities were in ruins,

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:52.723
they'd been bombed out, but
in 1950 they got together.

00:13:52.723 --> 00:13:55.306
They meaning industry and
government got together

00:13:55.306 --> 00:13:56.139
and set a goal.

00:13:56.139 --> 00:13:58.229
The goal was "we're going to
be the number one nation in

00:13:58.229 --> 00:14:02.905
"the world during the 1950s in
the production of textiles."

00:14:02.905 --> 00:14:05.811
In 1959, ladies and gentleman,

00:14:05.811 --> 00:14:08.063
they accomplished that objective.

00:14:08.063 --> 00:14:10.258
In 1960, they set another goal.

00:14:10.258 --> 00:14:12.528
"We're going to be the number
one nation in the world

00:14:12.528 --> 00:14:14.122
"in the production of steel."

00:14:14.122 --> 00:14:16.089
And, when you understand
there's no iron ore

00:14:16.089 --> 00:14:18.973
in Japan of any
significance, there's no coal

00:14:18.973 --> 00:14:21.197
of any significance
there, "We're going to be

00:14:21.197 --> 00:14:23.917
"the nation in the world in
the production of steel,"

00:14:23.917 --> 00:14:25.830
it seemed like an absurd goal.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.545
And yet, they reached their objectives.

00:14:28.545 --> 00:14:29.815
They had taken all of the steps.

00:14:29.815 --> 00:14:32.416
In 1970, the Japanese set another goal.

00:14:32.416 --> 00:14:34.630
They said, "During the 70s
we'll be the number one

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:38.024
"nation in the world in the
production of automobiles."

00:14:38.024 --> 00:14:40.755
They missed it folks, one year.

00:14:40.755 --> 00:14:42.265
It took them until 1980.

00:14:42.265 --> 00:14:44.224
In 1980 they set another goal.

00:14:44.224 --> 00:14:46.813
And, this time their goal
was, "We're going to be

00:14:46.813 --> 00:14:48.083
"the number one nation in the world

00:14:48.083 --> 00:14:51.198
"in the production of
computers and electronics."

00:14:51.198 --> 00:14:53.095
Yes indeed, ladies and gentlemen,

00:14:53.095 --> 00:14:56.178
you absolutely must have those goals.

00:14:57.198 --> 00:14:59.421
Let me share with you a true story out of

00:14:59.421 --> 00:15:01.418
my notebook of life.

00:15:01.418 --> 00:15:04.559
A number of years ago
got an incredible letter

00:15:04.559 --> 00:15:08.482
from a gentleman in Toronto
with a substantial check in it.

00:15:08.482 --> 00:15:11.293
He said, "My friend
Steve Walker is following

00:15:11.293 --> 00:15:13.126
"the wrong role model.

00:15:14.701 --> 00:15:17.949
"He's working himself to death,
his family's falling apart.

00:15:17.949 --> 00:15:20.524
"His health is in danger.

00:15:20.524 --> 00:15:22.738
"And, he's modeled himself out of his boss

00:15:22.738 --> 00:15:26.186
"and he trusts you and respects you.

00:15:26.186 --> 00:15:29.403
"If you will give us
one hour I will fly him

00:15:29.403 --> 00:15:32.877
"to Dallas and give you this check."

00:15:32.877 --> 00:15:35.429
Wouldn't you love to
have a friend like that?

00:15:35.429 --> 00:15:38.202
I sent the check back and
I said, "Come on down."

00:15:38.202 --> 00:15:40.654
Steve and I had quite a little talk.

00:15:40.654 --> 00:15:45.622
And, in our talk I asked him
why he had such a role model?

00:15:45.622 --> 00:15:49.462
What about this man that
made him so completely

00:15:49.462 --> 00:15:50.967
important in his life?

00:15:50.967 --> 00:15:53.334
And he said, "Well, he's
the most successful man

00:15:53.334 --> 00:15:55.453
"I have ever known."

00:15:55.453 --> 00:15:58.689
And I said, "Well Steve,
what do you call success?"

00:15:58.689 --> 00:16:01.464
Now, it took several minutes for him to go

00:16:01.464 --> 00:16:03.073
down the list.

00:16:03.073 --> 00:16:04.928
But, the interesting
thing is though their not

00:16:04.928 --> 00:16:07.656
in the same order that I have put them,

00:16:07.656 --> 00:16:10.222
or we'll list them now.

00:16:10.222 --> 00:16:13.262
These are the eight things that he said

00:16:13.262 --> 00:16:16.029
he really considered to be successful.

00:16:16.029 --> 00:16:19.646
If a man was happy and
healthy and at least

00:16:19.646 --> 00:16:22.470
reasonably prosperous and
secure, if he had friends,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.369
and peace of mind, and
good family relationships,

00:16:25.369 --> 00:16:28.170
and the hope that future's
going to be better

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.060
he said, "I consider
that man as successful."

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.874
Now, I'm going to really
encourage you to take some

00:16:33.874 --> 00:16:34.771
notes right here.

00:16:34.771 --> 00:16:36.628
And all I want you to do,
and you'll be the only one

00:16:36.628 --> 00:16:41.185
that'll see it somewhere
right as I go down the list

00:16:41.185 --> 00:16:43.380
write whether you get a plus on that one

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:45.873
or a minus on that one.

00:16:45.873 --> 00:16:47.553
You'll be the only one to see it,

00:16:47.553 --> 00:16:49.046
but it might be an eye opener.

00:16:49.046 --> 00:16:52.787
You see, most people never
dare to evaluate really

00:16:52.787 --> 00:16:54.629
where they are.

00:16:54.629 --> 00:16:56.603
And, you got to know
where you are before you

00:16:56.603 --> 00:16:59.044
can really determine
your chances of getting

00:16:59.044 --> 00:17:01.355
what you really want out of life.

00:17:01.355 --> 00:17:04.890
And so, I said to Steve,
I said, "I understand

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.521
"that your boss is very important to you.

00:17:07.521 --> 00:17:09.435
"You consider to him to be successful."

00:17:09.435 --> 00:17:11.467
When he finally got through
with the identification

00:17:11.467 --> 00:17:14.216
as I said earlier these
were the eight things

00:17:14.216 --> 00:17:17.168
that he said, "I think
that's important for success,

00:17:17.168 --> 00:17:18.650
"to measure success."

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:21.683
I said, "Well Steve, let
me ask you now as far as

00:17:21.683 --> 00:17:26.124
"happy's concerned tell
how happy is your boss?"

00:17:26.124 --> 00:17:28.467
He said, "Oh, I don't
think he's happy at all."

00:17:28.467 --> 00:17:30.113
I said, "Why not?"

00:17:30.113 --> 00:17:31.641
He said, "Well, I've never heard him laugh

00:17:31.641 --> 00:17:35.993
"and he seldom smiles and
besides that he's got ulcers."

00:17:35.993 --> 00:17:37.803
I said, "Well, do give
him a plus or a minus?"

00:17:37.803 --> 00:17:39.256
He said, "Oh, you give him a minus."

00:17:39.256 --> 00:17:41.183
Please grade yourself as we go.

00:17:41.183 --> 00:17:43.511
This is so important for you.

00:17:43.511 --> 00:17:46.267
Then I said, "That also
tells me something about

00:17:46.267 --> 00:17:48.560
"his health if he's got those ulcers.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.309
"Do I give him a plus or a minus?"

00:17:50.309 --> 00:17:52.246
He said, "You give him a minus."

00:17:52.246 --> 00:17:54.546
I said, "That also says something, Steve,

00:17:54.546 --> 00:17:57.006
"about his peace of mind because you don't

00:17:57.006 --> 00:17:58.845
"get ulcers because of what you eat

00:17:58.845 --> 00:17:59.865
"but what's eating you."

00:17:59.865 --> 00:18:01.514
I said, "Do we give
him a plus or a minus?"

00:18:01.514 --> 00:18:03.768
He said, "We give him a minus."

00:18:03.768 --> 00:18:05.640
I said, "Steve, I've asked
you one question about

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:08.451
"what success is, what
balance is, what's important

00:18:08.451 --> 00:18:11.532
"to you, one question
and your boss comes up

00:18:11.532 --> 00:18:12.746
"with three minuses."

00:18:12.746 --> 00:18:14.758
I said, "Tell me how prosperous he is."

00:18:14.758 --> 00:18:17.068
He said, "Man, he's got
money running out of

00:18:17.068 --> 00:18:19.555
"his ears and he's getting more everyday."

00:18:19.555 --> 00:18:21.753
I said, "We give him a plus
on that one, don't we?"

00:18:21.753 --> 00:18:23.498
He said, "We sure do."

00:18:23.498 --> 00:18:26.228
I said, "How secure is your boss?"

00:18:26.228 --> 00:18:29.715
He said, "Well, he's secure
as money can make you."

00:18:29.715 --> 00:18:32.677
And I said, "Well Steve,
let me ask you did you read

00:18:32.677 --> 00:18:34.588
"where two billionaire brothers here

00:18:34.588 --> 00:18:36.152
"in Dallas went bankrupt?"

00:18:36.152 --> 00:18:37.467
I said, "How does your boss compare?"

00:18:37.467 --> 00:18:40.246
"Oh man, he doesn't have
that kind of money."

00:18:40.246 --> 00:18:41.867
"Did you read where our
ex-governor was worth

00:18:41.867 --> 00:18:44.677
"a hundred million dollars at
one point, he's now bankrupt.

00:18:44.677 --> 00:18:46.309
"How does your boss compare?"

00:18:46.309 --> 00:18:48.793
"Oh, he doesn't have that kind of money."

00:18:48.793 --> 00:18:51.620
Now understand Steve equated security with

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.778
corporate position and bucks in the bank.

00:18:54.778 --> 00:18:57.878
So, I said, "Well Steve,
let me ask you would we

00:18:57.878 --> 00:19:00.359
"give your boss a plus or
a minus or just a question

00:19:00.359 --> 00:19:01.192
"mark on that?"

00:19:01.192 --> 00:19:04.189
He said, "Let's be generous
and give him a question mark."

00:19:04.189 --> 00:19:06.028
I said, "How many friends
does your boss have?"

00:19:06.028 --> 00:19:08.194
I hope you're marking these for you.

00:19:08.194 --> 00:19:12.112
"How many friends, real
friends does your boss have?"

00:19:12.112 --> 00:19:14.841
He said, "I don't think he's got any.

00:19:14.841 --> 00:19:17.072
"I'm not his friend I just
happen to admire the guy.

00:19:17.072 --> 00:19:19.439
"To tell you the truth
he's somewhat of a jerk."

00:19:19.439 --> 00:19:22.827
And, I said, "Okay, we give
him a minus on that one."

00:19:22.827 --> 00:19:24.450
I said, "Tell me about his family."

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:26.808
He said, "Well, his wife's divorcing him."

00:19:26.808 --> 00:19:28.917
"Well, that one's easy to answer."

00:19:28.917 --> 00:19:32.361
I said, "How much hope does
he have for the future?"

00:19:32.361 --> 00:19:34.073
He said, "Well, afore I
started talking to you

00:19:34.073 --> 00:19:35.169
"I thought he had a lot.

00:19:35.169 --> 00:19:37.890
(laughs)

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:40.819
"But, right now I don't
think he's got any."

00:19:40.819 --> 00:19:42.301
Why did that statement come forth?

00:19:42.301 --> 00:19:46.126
Because for the first
time he had evaluated it.

00:19:46.126 --> 00:19:48.987
That's what we need to
do, evaluate where we are.

00:19:48.987 --> 00:19:51.106
Are we investing our time properly,

00:19:51.106 --> 00:19:52.961
using our resources properly?

00:19:52.961 --> 00:19:55.773
What will the end results be?

00:19:55.773 --> 00:19:58.604
Well, then I said to
Steven when that was over,

00:19:58.604 --> 00:20:00.624
"We give him the minus there."

00:20:00.624 --> 00:20:03.561
I said, "Steve, of the eight
things that you consider

00:20:03.561 --> 00:20:06.750
"marks of success he gets one, two, three,

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.833
"four, five, six minuses.

00:20:09.813 --> 00:20:13.230
"He gets one plus and one question mark."

00:20:14.236 --> 00:20:16.207
I asked Steven a question,
I'm going to ask you

00:20:16.207 --> 00:20:17.707
the same question.

00:20:18.613 --> 00:20:21.028
"Steve, would you swap places
with your boss right now

00:20:21.028 --> 00:20:23.795
"knowing what you know?"

00:20:23.795 --> 00:20:26.221
He looked at me kind of stunned.

00:20:26.221 --> 00:20:28.811
He slowly rose to his feet and said,

00:20:28.811 --> 00:20:30.228
"No, I wouldn't."

00:20:32.323 --> 00:20:33.352
Would you?

00:20:33.352 --> 00:20:36.755
I never will forget this particular thing.

00:20:36.755 --> 00:20:40.583
On the radio interviews
I was having done and did

00:20:40.583 --> 00:20:44.526
at least 90% of the first 25 of them

00:20:44.526 --> 00:20:47.301
would always start with the same thing.

00:20:47.301 --> 00:20:50.916
"Zig, they tell me you could
sell anything to anybody."

00:20:50.916 --> 00:20:54.114
And I said, "yeah, they lied
to ya when they said that

00:20:54.114 --> 00:20:57.544
"because you've just
described a con artist."

00:20:57.544 --> 00:21:00.484
No legitimate professional sales person

00:21:00.484 --> 00:21:04.047
would ever dream of
selling anything that he

00:21:04.047 --> 00:21:07.087
did not fervently believe
was in the best interest

00:21:07.087 --> 00:21:09.531
of the person he was selling to.

00:21:09.531 --> 00:21:12.865
Of all of the qualities a
good sales person must have

00:21:12.865 --> 00:21:16.806
number one is integrity
because if they don't

00:21:16.806 --> 00:21:20.051
have that, see we are
trained as sales people

00:21:20.051 --> 00:21:24.170
to persuade other people
to buy, to make decisions.

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.997
And, an unethical sales
person can persuade people

00:21:27.997 --> 00:21:31.707
to buy things that they
don't need, are overpriced,

00:21:31.707 --> 00:21:34.580
or they pressure them into buying it.

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:38.822
A legitimate integrity driven sales person

00:21:38.822 --> 00:21:41.859
will only sell a product
that will enable him

00:21:41.859 --> 00:21:44.829
to sell them again and again and again,

00:21:44.829 --> 00:21:48.127
a product he would sell to
his mother, in other words.

00:21:48.127 --> 00:21:49.791
That's the kind of sales person who has

00:21:49.791 --> 00:21:52.548
long term balanced success.

00:21:52.548 --> 00:21:54.143
Now, unfortunately a lot
of people are standing

00:21:54.143 --> 00:21:55.416
I front of a stove a life and they say,

00:21:55.416 --> 00:21:56.573
"Now stove, you give me some heat then

00:21:56.573 --> 00:21:58.255
"I'll put some wood in you."

00:21:58.255 --> 00:21:59.493
That ain't the way it works.

00:21:59.493 --> 00:22:03.168
You got to put something in
before you can get anything out.

00:22:03.168 --> 00:22:05.251
So, many times you know
the employee goes to

00:22:05.251 --> 00:22:07.177
the employer and say,
"Give me a raise and then

00:22:07.177 --> 00:22:09.042
"I start coming to work on time."

00:22:09.042 --> 00:22:12.255
Or, so many times they
will come to 'em and say

00:22:12.255 --> 00:22:13.383
"Make me the boss.

00:22:13.383 --> 00:22:14.775
"Now, I know I haven't
been here very long,

00:22:14.775 --> 00:22:16.116
"don't really deserve to be the boss

00:22:16.116 --> 00:22:18.767
"but I just function better when I am

00:22:18.767 --> 00:22:19.772
"in charge of things.

00:22:19.772 --> 00:22:21.394
"You reward me now, then I promise you

00:22:21.394 --> 00:22:24.331
"I'll learn what this business
is all about later on.

00:22:24.331 --> 00:22:27.088
"Reward me now and I'll produce later."

00:22:27.088 --> 00:22:28.289
Doesn't work that way.

00:22:28.289 --> 00:22:30.495
Can't you just see a
youngster in a school saying,

00:22:30.495 --> 00:22:32.736
"Teacher, if I take a
failing grade home my

00:22:32.736 --> 00:22:34.551
"parents are going to skin me alive.

00:22:34.551 --> 00:22:37.270
"Pass me on this quarter
and next time I'll study

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:38.476
"more than anybody else.

00:22:38.476 --> 00:22:40.764
"Reward me now, I'll produce later."

00:22:40.764 --> 00:22:41.840
It doesn't work that way.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.244
Can't you just see an
old farmer standing out

00:22:44.244 --> 00:22:46.006
in the fields in October
and saying, "Lord, I know

00:22:46.006 --> 00:22:47.849
"I didn't plant a thing
this year, but if you'll

00:22:47.849 --> 00:22:50.021
"give me a big crop this
year I'll plant more

00:22:50.021 --> 00:22:52.556
"than anybody next year."

00:22:52.556 --> 00:22:55.977
It ain't that way folks,
you got to put something in

00:22:55.977 --> 00:22:58.882
before you can expect to get anything out.

00:22:58.882 --> 00:23:00.341
Well, he's just a pumping away,

00:23:00.341 --> 00:23:01.898
"that's hard, it's all good."

00:23:01.898 --> 00:23:05.314
I mean, the question is
how much pumping are you

00:23:05.314 --> 00:23:07.479
going to to do before I drink the water.

00:23:07.479 --> 00:23:09.161
And finally, old Bernard
said, "you know Jimmy,

00:23:09.161 --> 00:23:10.473
"I don't believe there's
any water down there."

00:23:10.473 --> 00:23:12.081
Jimmy said, "Yeah there is, Bernard."

00:23:12.081 --> 00:23:13.876
You know, in south
Alabama the wells are deep

00:23:13.876 --> 00:23:16.081
and oh we're glad they're
deep because the deeper

00:23:16.081 --> 00:23:17.745
the well the cooler, the
cleaner, the sweeter,

00:23:17.745 --> 00:23:20.207
the purer, the better tasting the water.

00:23:20.207 --> 00:23:22.206
And, isn't that true of life?

00:23:22.206 --> 00:23:24.237
Isn't it true that if
you can become an M.D.

00:23:24.237 --> 00:23:26.297
by six weeks of summer
school that the rewards

00:23:26.297 --> 00:23:29.035
would be almost minimal
or nothing and how many

00:23:29.035 --> 00:23:31.102
patients would you have?

00:23:31.102 --> 00:23:33.594
Isn't it true if you become a sales expert

00:23:33.594 --> 00:23:36.652
in three days of a training
school that the rate

00:23:36.652 --> 00:23:39.017
of pay would go down rather radically?

00:23:39.017 --> 00:23:42.159
Isn't it true that anything worth doing

00:23:42.159 --> 00:23:43.909
is worth doing poorly

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:48.263
until you can learn to do it well?

00:23:49.517 --> 00:23:51.452
We'll never know how many kids have missed

00:23:51.452 --> 00:23:53.449
a college scholarship
because they didn't study

00:23:53.449 --> 00:23:55.774
an average of 10 more minutes a day.

00:23:55.774 --> 00:23:59.412
We will never know how we
come so close to a promotion

00:23:59.412 --> 00:24:01.974
but we're get discouraged
and quit too soon.

00:24:01.974 --> 00:24:04.764
We'll never know how much
more success we would

00:24:04.764 --> 00:24:08.783
have had had we just had a
little more pumping in there

00:24:08.783 --> 00:24:11.273
and pumping, pumping, pumping, pumping.

00:24:11.273 --> 00:24:13.805
Well, finally Old Bernard
just got disgusted

00:24:13.805 --> 00:24:15.011
threw up his hands he
said, "Jimmy, there's just

00:24:15.011 --> 00:24:16.004
"no water down there."

00:24:16.004 --> 00:24:18.295
Jimmy said, "Don't stop
Bernard, don't stop

00:24:18.295 --> 00:24:20.364
"if you stop the water's
going to go all the way

00:24:20.364 --> 00:24:22.017
"back down and then
you're going to have to

00:24:22.017 --> 00:24:23.434
"start all over."

00:24:25.361 --> 00:24:28.134
The reality is folks, and I'm
totally convinced of this,

00:24:28.134 --> 00:24:30.672
this is the story of
America, this is your story.

00:24:30.672 --> 00:24:31.862
This is the story of success.

00:24:31.862 --> 00:24:33.402
This is the story of life.

00:24:33.402 --> 00:24:35.469
I believe with all of my
heart that if you will

00:24:35.469 --> 00:24:37.898
pump long enough and hard enough and

00:24:37.898 --> 00:24:40.579
enthusiastically enough
that eventually the reward

00:24:40.579 --> 00:24:42.819
is going to follow the effort.

00:24:42.819 --> 00:24:47.482
And then, once that water
starts to flow all you

00:24:47.482 --> 00:24:51.367
got to do is just keep a
little easy, steady pressure

00:24:51.367 --> 00:24:53.333
on it and you're going to get more water

00:24:53.333 --> 00:24:55.965
than you can possibly use.

00:24:55.965 --> 00:24:59.450
The basic problem is
this so many times people

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:01.436
get involved in something and then say,

00:25:01.436 --> 00:25:04.185
"Well, I'll give it a
try and if it works out

00:25:04.185 --> 00:25:07.006
"that'll be good and if doesn't work out

00:25:07.006 --> 00:25:08.476
"I mean, hey I'm not going to kill myself.

00:25:08.476 --> 00:25:09.974
"You know what I mean, feller?"

00:25:09.974 --> 00:25:11.833
Well, I got to tell you something folks

00:25:11.833 --> 00:25:13.810
you're going to pump forever like that

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:14.829
before anything happens.

00:25:14.829 --> 00:25:16.675
When you get into
something grab that sucker

00:25:16.675 --> 00:25:18.269
and get with it.

00:25:18.269 --> 00:25:21.578
And then, once the water starts to flow

00:25:21.578 --> 00:25:23.263
then, ladies and gentlemen, that's what

00:25:23.263 --> 00:25:26.096
strategy for success is all about.

00:25:28.297 --> 00:25:29.996
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:25:29.996 --> 00:25:33.235
I made this video because
Alvinladen asked me to.

00:25:33.235 --> 00:25:34.920
So, if there's a famous
entrepreneur you want

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.453
me to profile next leave
it in the comments below

00:25:37.453 --> 00:25:38.703
and I'll see what I can do.

00:25:38.703 --> 00:25:41.782
I'd also love to know which
of Zig's top ten rules

00:25:41.782 --> 00:25:44.484
meant the most to you, what
impact it had on your life,

00:25:44.484 --> 00:25:46.119
what change your going
to make now as a result

00:25:46.119 --> 00:25:47.519
of watching this video.

00:25:47.519 --> 00:25:48.913
I'm really curious to find out.

00:25:48.913 --> 00:25:51.660
Leave it in the comments below
and I'll join the discussion.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.393
Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:25:53.393 --> 00:25:56.243
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:25:56.243 --> 00:26:00.410
(air rushes)
(thuds)

00:26:02.294 --> 00:26:06.516
- You need to read at
least 20 minutes every day.

00:26:06.516 --> 00:26:09.752
Now, I spend about two
hours a day or more,

00:26:09.752 --> 00:26:12.084
but you got to understand
this is my business,

00:26:12.084 --> 00:26:14.560
this is my life, this is my profession.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:15.746
I got to do it.

00:26:15.746 --> 00:26:19.420
Fortunately, I just
absolutely love to do it.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:22.728
But, 20 minutes a day will
make a dramatic difference.

00:26:22.728 --> 00:26:26.760
Now obviously, I'm talking
about reading something good,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:30.843
powerful, positive,
biographies, autobiographies,

00:26:31.768 --> 00:26:35.464
self-help books, motivational
books, books about

00:26:35.464 --> 00:26:38.136
your business, your
profession, the industry

00:26:38.136 --> 00:26:41.516
of which you are a part,
books about human nature,

00:26:41.516 --> 00:26:43.405
how to deal with people.

00:26:43.405 --> 00:26:48.217
If you are an average reader,
that's 220 words a minute

00:26:48.217 --> 00:26:51.848
if you will just read 20 minutes a day

00:26:51.848 --> 00:26:54.733
at the end of the year
you will have read 20

00:26:54.733 --> 00:26:55.983
200 page books.

00:26:57.639 --> 00:27:00.306
That is 18 more than the average

00:27:02.638 --> 00:27:05.305
American reads in a year's time.

00:27:07.603 --> 00:27:11.336
That will give you a colossal advantage

00:27:11.336 --> 00:27:14.375
in whatever it is that you're going.

00:27:14.375 --> 00:27:17.020
I listened to the people
who were successful

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:18.224
in selling their product.

00:27:18.224 --> 00:27:20.264
I would learn new things
from different ones,

00:27:20.264 --> 00:27:22.606
new approaches and
that's where you must be

00:27:22.606 --> 00:27:24.548
a constant student.

00:27:24.548 --> 00:27:27.107
A lot of people are
afraid to try anything new

00:27:27.107 --> 00:27:30.699
because they are afraid that
they will be embarrassed

00:27:30.699 --> 00:27:32.287
until they get good at it.

00:27:32.287 --> 00:27:35.204
Well, one of my favorite sayings is

00:27:36.467 --> 00:27:38.292
the most important thing you can do is

00:27:38.292 --> 00:27:41.355
to understand that anything worth doing

00:27:41.355 --> 00:27:42.825
is worth doing poorly.

00:27:42.825 --> 00:27:43.658
(laughs)

00:27:43.658 --> 00:27:44.491
- What do you mean by that?

00:27:44.491 --> 00:27:47.786
- Until you can learn to do it well.

00:27:47.786 --> 00:27:50.708
My first few sales calls were pathetic,

00:27:50.708 --> 00:27:53.879
but I knew it was worth
doing so I kept after it.

00:27:53.879 --> 00:27:56.632
My first few speeches
were a disaster but I

00:27:56.632 --> 00:27:59.501
believed that I could develop into that.

00:27:59.501 --> 00:28:03.182
So, I kept at it until I
could do it effectively.

00:28:03.182 --> 00:28:05.545
And, all skills are exactly the same way.

00:28:05.545 --> 00:28:08.762
No running back that sets the record ever

00:28:08.762 --> 00:28:11.144
set a record the first time
he picked up a football.

00:28:11.144 --> 00:28:13.551
And, you can go the right
down the line with that.

00:28:13.551 --> 00:28:16.455
So, if it's worth doing
and you believe in it

00:28:16.455 --> 00:28:17.991
go ahead and fail.

00:28:17.991 --> 00:28:19.350
And, the more times you fail the closer

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:21.532
you get into success because you should be

00:28:21.532 --> 00:28:24.630
learning on every simple
thing that you do.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:26.136
A number of years ago Laurie Majors,

00:28:26.136 --> 00:28:28.504
my executive assistant, got a phone call

00:28:28.504 --> 00:28:30.837
from a lady in Birmingham, Alabama.

00:28:30.837 --> 00:28:32.515
At the end of the conversation she said,

00:28:32.515 --> 00:28:35.343
"Zig," she said, "I
believe this woman thinks

00:28:35.343 --> 00:28:38.061
"she's got an impossible
problem, but I believe

00:28:38.061 --> 00:28:40.595
"you can solve that
problem with her in just

00:28:40.595 --> 00:28:43.540
"a few minutes if you will
spend that time with her."

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:46.394
I said, "Well Laurie, tell
her to meet me back stage,

00:28:46.394 --> 00:28:48.072
"I'll get there about 10 minutes early."

00:28:48.072 --> 00:28:50.959
My schedule was such that
that was about all I had.

00:28:50.959 --> 00:28:53.958
Well, I got there and
I was backstage behind

00:28:53.958 --> 00:28:57.149
the curtain on one side she
spotted me from the other side

00:28:57.149 --> 00:28:59.970
and as she walked across
the stage I have never seen

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:02.687
as much anger in a human being in my life

00:29:02.687 --> 00:29:04.362
as I saw in her.

00:29:04.362 --> 00:29:06.218
She almost started crying when she saw me.

00:29:06.218 --> 00:29:08.265
She said, "oh, I'm just
so glad to see you.

00:29:08.265 --> 00:29:09.928
"I got this horrible job, I hate it.

00:29:09.928 --> 00:29:10.988
"I hate everything about it.

00:29:10.988 --> 00:29:12.308
"I hate everybody down there."

00:29:12.308 --> 00:29:16.495
I mean, you're talking about
negative Nell, she was it.

00:29:16.495 --> 00:29:18.411
She said, "Can you help me?"

00:29:18.411 --> 00:29:21.282
Now, understand I've only
got about 10 minutes.

00:29:21.282 --> 00:29:24.514
So, I looked at her and
one thing I have learned

00:29:24.514 --> 00:29:27.192
I don't do counseling,
but I talk with a lot

00:29:27.192 --> 00:29:30.498
of people who do in
psychologists, psychiatry,

00:29:30.498 --> 00:29:31.701
and the ministry.

00:29:31.701 --> 00:29:33.744
And, they tell me that
everybody who comes to you

00:29:33.744 --> 00:29:36.117
with a problem are not necessarily looking

00:29:36.117 --> 00:29:38.185
for a solution.

00:29:38.185 --> 00:29:39.893
I couldn't understand
that for a long time.

00:29:39.893 --> 00:29:41.366
Why do they bring you a problem if they

00:29:41.366 --> 00:29:42.402
don't want to solve it?

00:29:42.402 --> 00:29:43.618
Well, I can tell you why.

00:29:43.618 --> 00:29:45.255
They want to tell you
about it, you about it,

00:29:45.255 --> 00:29:46.923
you about it, you about
it, and you about it

00:29:46.923 --> 00:29:49.274
and if you foul up the
deal and solve the problem

00:29:49.274 --> 00:29:51.677
they can't tell you again, you again.

00:29:51.677 --> 00:29:55.024
They want the attention
that goes with the problem.

00:29:55.024 --> 00:29:57.586
And every company just about it has

00:29:57.586 --> 00:29:59.361
that kind of an individual.

00:29:59.361 --> 00:30:01.379
They want the attention that goes with

00:30:01.379 --> 00:30:03.995
griping and complaining.

00:30:03.995 --> 00:30:07.933
Well, I looked at the lady
and I wasn't unkindly,

00:30:07.933 --> 00:30:10.125
but firmly I said to her,
"Yes, and you know ma'am

00:30:10.125 --> 00:30:13.269
"I'm afraid your problem
is about to get worse."

00:30:13.269 --> 00:30:15.171
She said, "What do you mean?"

00:30:15.171 --> 00:30:17.427
I said, "I believe they're
going to fire you."

00:30:17.427 --> 00:30:19.510
(laughs)

00:30:21.209 --> 00:30:22.345
She was stunned.

00:30:22.345 --> 00:30:23.320
I couldn't have stunned her more if I

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:25.459
had hit her in the face
with a bucket of ice water.

00:30:25.459 --> 00:30:29.320
She said, "Fire me, why on
Earth would they fire me?"

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:31.363
The inflection in her
voice clearly said "they're

00:30:31.363 --> 00:30:33.423
"the bad guys, I'm the good guy.

00:30:33.423 --> 00:30:36.268
"Why don't they fire them and keep me?"

00:30:36.268 --> 00:30:38.640
Have you ever noticed that
people who are the problem

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:41.736
never recognize that they are?

00:30:41.736 --> 00:30:43.513
They're in complete denial.

00:30:43.513 --> 00:30:46.433
They think denial is
just a river in Egypt.

00:30:46.433 --> 00:30:47.763
(laughs)

00:30:47.763 --> 00:30:49.815
"Why would they fire me?"

00:30:49.815 --> 00:30:51.668
I said, "Ma'am, I don't
believe there's a company

00:30:51.668 --> 00:30:54.087
"in America big enough
to contain this much

00:30:54.087 --> 00:30:56.062
"poison in one small spot."

00:30:56.062 --> 00:30:57.451
(laughs)

00:30:57.451 --> 00:31:00.095
Have you ever noticed
that when somebody's about

00:31:00.095 --> 00:31:02.942
to lose something they've
been complaining about

00:31:02.942 --> 00:31:07.659
whether it's a car, a home,
a mate, a job, or whatever,

00:31:07.659 --> 00:31:09.616
when all of a sudden it appears
they're going to lose 'em

00:31:09.616 --> 00:31:11.949
it takes on brand new value.

00:31:13.122 --> 00:31:16.289
She looked at me and said,
"Well, what can I do?"

00:31:16.289 --> 00:31:17.598
I said, "Do you really want to know?"

00:31:17.598 --> 00:31:20.214
She said, "Yes, I do that's
the reason I came to see you.

00:31:20.214 --> 00:31:22.113
"I came looking for help
but you sure haven't

00:31:22.113 --> 00:31:23.379
"been any help so far."

00:31:23.379 --> 00:31:24.457
(laughs)

00:31:24.457 --> 00:31:27.086
I said, "Well ma'am, I've
got an idea and I will

00:31:27.086 --> 00:31:31.003
"absolutely guarantee you
it positively, definitely

00:31:31.003 --> 00:31:33.598
"absolutely will work
if you will just do it."

00:31:33.598 --> 00:31:36.199
She said, "I'll try
anything, within reason."

00:31:36.199 --> 00:31:38.797
I said, "Okay, when you get home tonight

00:31:38.797 --> 00:31:41.209
"all of your household tasks are complete,

00:31:41.209 --> 00:31:44.812
"it's bedtime get off in
a room right by yourself,

00:31:44.812 --> 00:31:47.088
"get a sheet of paper
out and at the top of it

00:31:47.088 --> 00:31:49.226
"write I like my job because"

00:31:49.226 --> 00:31:50.942
She interrupted me, she
said, "That'll be easy.

00:31:50.942 --> 00:31:52.683
"I don't like nothing about that job.

00:31:52.683 --> 00:31:54.753
"Don't like nothing about
those people down there."

00:31:54.753 --> 00:31:56.238
(laughs)

00:31:56.238 --> 00:31:58.154
And, I said, "Just as
a matter of curiosity

00:31:58.154 --> 00:32:01.859
"do you work there for benevolent
reasons or do they pay you

00:32:01.859 --> 00:32:03.613
"for working there?"

00:32:03.613 --> 00:32:05.775
She said, "Well, I got
to confess, they pay me."

00:32:05.775 --> 00:32:07.726
And said, "You don't like to be paid."

00:32:07.726 --> 00:32:09.492
Oh, she said, "Yes, I do."

00:32:09.492 --> 00:32:12.051
I said, "Okay, tell you
what you do open your

00:32:12.051 --> 00:32:14.331
"notebook right now
we'll start our list of

00:32:14.331 --> 00:32:17.112
"the things you like about
your job, they pay you

00:32:17.112 --> 00:32:18.992
"for working there and you
do like it, don't you?"

00:32:18.992 --> 00:32:20.464
She said, "Absolutely."

00:32:20.464 --> 00:32:21.297
But, she just stood there.

00:32:21.297 --> 00:32:24.137
I said, "No, open your
notebook now and we'll get

00:32:24.137 --> 00:32:25.598
"busy on the list."

00:32:25.598 --> 00:32:27.199
She just stood there.

00:32:27.199 --> 00:32:29.762
I said, "Ma'am, let me
tell you what my experience

00:32:29.762 --> 00:32:31.385
"in life has been.

00:32:31.385 --> 00:32:34.121
"I've discovered that
in 100% of the cases,

00:32:34.121 --> 00:32:38.787
"no exception, people who
won't take step number one

00:32:38.787 --> 00:32:42.030
"never take step number two."

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:44.889
You see, she had come to me
with an impossible dream.

00:32:44.889 --> 00:32:47.329
Her dream was that nice
Mister Zigler was going to

00:32:47.329 --> 00:32:49.645
solve all of her problems
and she would live

00:32:49.645 --> 00:32:51.228
happily ever after.

00:32:52.259 --> 00:32:55.706
But folks, I got news for you
I can't solve her problems.

00:32:55.706 --> 00:32:57.893
I can't solve your problems.

00:32:57.893 --> 00:32:59.611
But, I will give you some steps that I'll

00:32:59.611 --> 00:33:03.778
absolutely, definitely, and
positively will work for you

00:33:04.917 --> 00:33:08.073
as it worked eventually for her.

00:33:08.073 --> 00:33:10.165
I said, "Well ma'am, let
me tell you something.

00:33:10.165 --> 00:33:12.209
"Unless you're willing
to take step number one

00:33:12.209 --> 00:33:15.296
"right now it's been
nice talking with you."

00:33:15.296 --> 00:33:18.462
She angrily opened her notebook.

00:33:18.462 --> 00:33:21.490
Before we got through there were 22 things

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:23.545
she liked about her job.

00:33:23.545 --> 00:33:26.185
Not only did they pay
her for working there

00:33:26.185 --> 00:33:27.927
they paid her above average.

00:33:27.927 --> 00:33:30.078
She had three weeks vacation with pay.

00:33:30.078 --> 00:33:31.982
She had a retirement program.

00:33:31.982 --> 00:33:33.657
She was in on profit sharing.

00:33:33.657 --> 00:33:35.340
She had health insurance, life insurance,

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:36.995
and an accident insurance.

00:33:36.995 --> 00:33:38.878
She lived less than 10 minutes from home.

00:33:38.878 --> 00:33:40.750
She was in on management decisions.

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:42.597
The company set her to
three seminars a year

00:33:42.597 --> 00:33:43.591
to be paid for.

00:33:43.591 --> 00:33:45.820
She had her own private
office and parking place.

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:49.529
22 things that she liked about her job.

00:33:49.529 --> 00:33:51.652
Now, I said, "Ma'am,
when you get home tonight

00:33:51.652 --> 00:33:53.610
"everything is finished, get off in a room

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:57.741
"right by yourself, close
the doors, change one

00:33:57.741 --> 00:34:01.241
"word from I like my job to I love my job.

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:04.470
"Get in front of that mirror,"

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:06.862
and folks I cannot say
this strongly enough,

00:34:06.862 --> 00:34:08.199
but I'm going to try.

00:34:08.199 --> 00:34:11.282
The eyes are the windows of the soul.

00:34:12.881 --> 00:34:15.779
Look yourself in the
eye and with excitement

00:34:15.779 --> 00:34:18.782
and enthusiasm say, "I
love my job because they

00:34:18.782 --> 00:34:20.171
"pay me for working there.

00:34:20.171 --> 00:34:21.531
"I love my job because they pay me

00:34:21.531 --> 00:34:23.062
"above average for working there.

00:34:23.062 --> 00:34:24.242
"I love my job because they have

00:34:24.242 --> 00:34:25.664
"a wonderful insurance program.

00:34:25.664 --> 00:34:30.060
"I love my job before every
one of the statements."

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:32.879
You will sleep better that night.

00:34:32.879 --> 00:34:34.429
You see, there's something hidden in what

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:35.840
I'm saying to you now.

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:38.349
When she says I like my
job she's really saying,

00:34:38.349 --> 00:34:41.130
"I'm grateful for my job."

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:44.066
And, of all of the emotions
we can have according to

00:34:44.066 --> 00:34:48.073
Ann Selia the number one
stress specialist in America,

00:34:48.073 --> 00:34:51.884
the healthiest of all human
emotions is gratitude.

00:34:51.884 --> 00:34:54.854
I said, "You go down
that list, I like my job,

00:34:54.854 --> 00:34:56.689
"I love my job, rather.

00:34:56.689 --> 00:34:58.371
"That is a way of gratitude.

00:34:58.371 --> 00:35:00.467
"You'll sleep better the first night.

00:35:00.467 --> 00:35:03.254
"Tomorrow morning when you get up get back

00:35:03.254 --> 00:35:06.218
"in front of the mirror
just before you go to work

00:35:06.218 --> 00:35:08.775
"get back in front of the
mirror and repeat the process

00:35:08.775 --> 00:35:11.111
"again with excitement and enthusiasm.

00:35:11.111 --> 00:35:14.771
"I love my job because, and
take the list with you."

00:35:14.771 --> 00:35:18.258
Because, the reality is,
you see, you will have

00:35:18.258 --> 00:35:22.425
started a change from a fault
finder to a good finder.

00:35:23.598 --> 00:35:25.657
Some people really do
find fault like there's

00:35:25.657 --> 00:35:27.793
a reward for it, they really do.

00:35:27.793 --> 00:35:30.599
Take the list with you
and you will be able

00:35:30.599 --> 00:35:34.606
to add to that list absolutely guaranteed.

00:35:34.606 --> 00:35:38.183
Do this every morning and every night

00:35:38.183 --> 00:35:40.763
and you will have an astonishing recovery

00:35:40.763 --> 00:35:43.823
from this advanced case
of stinkin' thinkin'.

00:35:43.823 --> 00:35:46.749
Now, I didn't say that to
her, but I'm saying it to you.

00:35:46.749 --> 00:35:48.017
That's what it was.

00:35:48.017 --> 00:35:50.761
It was an advanced case
of stinkin' thinkin'.

00:35:50.761 --> 00:35:54.023
Well, six weeks later I was
back in Birmingham, Alabama.

00:35:54.023 --> 00:35:57.418
I was doing a follow up sales seminar.

00:35:57.418 --> 00:36:00.931
Now, the lady was not in
sales, but she had been

00:36:00.931 --> 00:36:02.878
listening to my tapes,
she had been listening

00:36:02.878 --> 00:36:04.808
to Automobile University.

00:36:04.808 --> 00:36:08.183
And, she had discovered
that everybody sells.

00:36:08.183 --> 00:36:11.530
Everybody who will ever
hear this is in selling

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:13.904
whether you're a school
teacher, a civil service

00:36:13.904 --> 00:36:17.237
worker, a military personnel,
an executive secretary

00:36:17.237 --> 00:36:19.097
it doesn't make any
difference what you do.

00:36:19.097 --> 00:36:22.032
You sell every day of your life.

00:36:22.032 --> 00:36:25.245
There she was at the
sales seminar seated on

00:36:25.245 --> 00:36:27.431
the front row, grinning
so wide she could have

00:36:27.431 --> 00:36:29.126
eaten a banana sideways.

00:36:29.126 --> 00:36:31.592
I'm tell ya' you're talking about somebody

00:36:31.592 --> 00:36:34.439
that was excited and she was turned on.

00:36:34.439 --> 00:36:36.469
I said, "Well, how you doing?"

00:36:36.469 --> 00:36:38.502
She grinned even more broadly and said,

00:36:38.502 --> 00:36:41.402
"Mister Zigler I'm doing wonderfully well

00:36:41.402 --> 00:36:43.904
"and thank you for asking."

00:36:43.904 --> 00:36:46.745
She said, "You cannot
believe how much those people

00:36:46.745 --> 00:36:48.029
"down there have changed."

00:36:48.029 --> 00:36:50.112
(laughs)

00:36:54.898 --> 00:36:57.516
I got to lay it the line folks,
you're not going to change

00:36:57.516 --> 00:37:00.662
anybody else till you change you.

00:37:00.662 --> 00:37:03.802
Everything really does begin with you.

00:37:03.802 --> 00:37:05.579
Now, you see, the
unfortunate thing this lady

00:37:05.579 --> 00:37:07.946
had been raised in a very
negative environment.

00:37:07.946 --> 00:37:09.536
First, her parents had
told her she'd never

00:37:09.536 --> 00:37:10.953
amount to anything.

00:37:10.953 --> 00:37:13.682
They said, "You're always
late, you're always sloppy.

00:37:13.682 --> 00:37:14.901
"Why can't you be like your brother

00:37:14.901 --> 00:37:16.957
"or you sister or whatever."

00:37:16.957 --> 00:37:19.375
When she got mad her
husband had contended it

00:37:19.375 --> 00:37:23.542
and so her self-talk had
become completely negative.

00:37:25.195 --> 00:37:27.753
Everything that she said
about herself was negative.

00:37:27.753 --> 00:37:30.245
"Like dad said, I'll
never amount to anything.

00:37:30.245 --> 00:37:33.329
"Or, like my husband says
I can't do anything right."

00:37:33.329 --> 00:37:36.009
But, when she started changing the input

00:37:36.009 --> 00:37:38.541
then some radical changes took place.

00:37:38.541 --> 00:37:39.374
(digital tones)

00:37:39.374 --> 00:37:41.533
- [Announcer] Zigler, Zigler

00:37:41.533 --> 00:37:43.783
inspiring true performance.

